 does not destroy coverage because each vertical line adds the whole column of points with that \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate, which were already covered by the sunny line that has been removed.  Hence for every admissible \\(k\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for a sunny line (even \\(n\\))\n\nAssume \\(n\\) is even and let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the side \\(x+y=n+1\\) of the triangle\n\\(\\operatorname{conv}(T_n)\\), it meets that side in a single point.\nConsequently \\(\\ell\\) meets exactly two of the three sides\n\\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) meets the sides \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) then its slope is\nnegative; if it meets \\(x=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) its slope is positive,\nand similarly for the other pair.\n\nThe integer points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\) form an arithmetic\nprogression with common difference equal to the denominator of the reduced\nslope of \\(\\ell\\).  The longest such progression occurs when the slope\nis \\(1\\); then the points are \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\frac n2,\\frac n2\\bigr)\\),\nso at most \\(\\frac n2\\) points of \\(T_n\\) lie on a sunny line.\nHence for even \\(n\\)\n\n\\[\n\\max_{\\ell\\text{ sunny}}|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le\\frac n2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf we had \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\), the total number of covered\npoints would be at most \\(n\\cdot\\frac n2=\\frac{n^{2}}2\\), whereas\n\\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>\\frac{n^{2}}2\\).  This contradiction shows\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For even }n,\\;k=n\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for odd \\(n\\) (exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Define the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{0}&:\\;y=x,\\\\[2mm]\nL_{i}&:\\;\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,n+2-i),\n\\qquad 2\\le i\\le n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{n+1-i}{i-1}\\).  \nBecause \\(n\\) is odd, the equality \\(\\dfrac{n+1-i}{i-1}=1\\) would give\n\\(i=\\dfrac{n+2}{2}\\), which is not an integer; therefore no \\(L_i\\) has\nslope \\(-1\\).  None of the lines is horizontal or vertical, so all\n\\(L_i\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{0}\\).\n\n* Suppose \\(a<b\\).  Put \\(i=b\\) (note that \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) because\n\\(b\\ge2\\)).  The points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,n+2-i)\\) lie on \\(L_i\\); the\nequation of \\(L_i\\) can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny-1=-\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}\\,(x-i).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(i=b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a=n+2-i-b=n+2-i-a\\); substituting\n\\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) into (4) yields an identity, so \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\n\n* The case \\(a>b\\) is symmetric (take \\(i=a\\)).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of the lines in (3).  Hence for\nodd \\(n\\) we have a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for even \\(n\\) (exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(n=2m\\;(m\\ge2)\\).  Apply the construction of \u00a72 to the odd integer\n\\(n-1=2m-1\\); we obtain \\(n-1\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{n-1}\\).\nThe points of \\(T_n\\setminus T_{n-1}\\) are precisely the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,n)\\in D\\).  Replace the sunny line \\(L_{0}\\) (the line\n\\(y=x\\)) by the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  This vertical line contains the\nwhole set \\(D\\) because every point of \\(D\\) has a distinct \\(x\\)-coordinate\nbetween \\(1\\) and \\(n\\); in particular it contains \\((1,n)\\).  The remaining\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines still cover all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\), and together\nwith the vertical line they cover all of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently we have a\nconfiguration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines for every even \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Obtaining every intermediate value of \\(k\\)\n\nLet a configuration with the maximal admissible number of sunny lines be\navailable (the one built in \u00a72 for odd \\(n\\) and the one of \u00a73 for even\n\\(n\\)).  To obtain a configuration with any smaller non\u2011negative integer\n\\(k\\) (still respecting the parity restriction of (2)), replace\n\\(n-k\\) of the sunny lines by distinct vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  Each vertical line adds the whole column of points\nwith that \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate, which were already covered by the sunny line\nthat has been removed; therefore coverage is preserved.  The replaced\nlines are non\u2011sunny, so the new configuration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Summary of the attainable values of \\(k\\)\n\n* If \\(n\\) is odd, the construction of \u00a72 gives a configuration with\n\\(k=n\\); by the replacement procedure we obtain configurations for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n* If \\(n\\) is even, \u00a73 provides a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\); the same\nreplacement yields configurations for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\nThe impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\) follows from (2).\n\nThus the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated\nin the boxed answer. \u220e"
