            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the condition “\\(a+b\\le n+1\\)” becomes \\(a+b\\le4\\).  \nThe positive integer solutions are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{horizontal:}&\\; y=c,\\\\\n\\text{vertical:}&\\; x=c,\\\\\n\\text{diagonal of slope }-1:&\\; x+y=c,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(c\\) is a real constant.  \nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\nWe must exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le3\\), three distinct lines\n\\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny and every point of\n\\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the three lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(k=0\\) (all three lines non‑sunny)\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=4 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach line is of the form (2), hence non‑sunny.  \nThe incidences are listed in the table below; every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is\ncovered.\n\n| \\((a,b)\\) | on \\(L_{1}\\) (\\(x=1\\))? | on \\(L_{2}\\) (\\(y=1\\))? | on \\(L_{3}\\) (\\(x+y=4\\))? | covered |\n|-----------|------------------------|------------------------|--------------------------|---------|\n| (1,