            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(L_i\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(L_i\\) are *sunny*, i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A counting bound for sunny lines\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal) or to the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) can also contain at most \\(n\\) points (the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points).\n\nConsider a sunny line \\(L\\).  Its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nWrite the line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(L\\) then \\(b=ma+b\\).  Because both coordinates are integers, \\(m\\) must be rational; write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\).  Then\n\n\\[\nqb = pa + qb \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q(b-a)=p a .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the integer \\(a\\) must be a multiple of \\(q\\).  Consequently the points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on a fixed sunny line are spaced at least \\(q\\) units apart in the \\(x\\)-direction, and similarly in the \\(y\\)-direction.  Inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) this forces\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap T_n\\bigr)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(The bound is attained for the line of slope \\(1\\), i.e. \\(y=x+c\\); it contains the points \\((1,1+c),\\,(2,2+c),\\dots\\) until the sum exceeds \\(n+1\\).)\n\nIf **all** \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of covered points would be at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\),\n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\begin{cases}\n<\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[4pt]\n=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFor even \\(n\\) inequality (5) is strict, so covering all points with only sunny lines is impossible.  \nFor odd \\(n\\) equality would require **every** sunny line to contain exactly \\((n+1)/2\\) points, but the only line achieving that many points is \\(y=x\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would contain fewer points, making the total strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=n\\text{ is impossible for all }n\\ge3}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x+i .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(S_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.  They are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is vertical, hence non‑sunny, and the \\(V_j\\) are distinct.\n\nWe now prove that the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=0}^{k-1}\\cup\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{\\,n-k}\\) cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_{\\,b-a}\\) and lies on that sunny line.\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  \nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-(b-a)}2\\le\\frac{n+1-k}2\\le n-k .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(The last inequality uses \\(k\\le n-1\\).)  \nThus \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-k\\); consequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIn both cases the point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  Hence the covering condition holds.\n\nThe families (7) and (8) are disjoint, so the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).  The total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nTherefore for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the existence result of Section 2 with the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) proved in Section 1, we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n*All steps have been justified rigorously, and every required point has been verified to lie on at least one of the constructed lines.*\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
