                "content": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have solved the problem completely.  \nThe set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;\\text{ for every }n\\ge 3,\\quad\\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e.  \n\n* if \\(n\\) is even, the possible values are \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\);  \n* if \\(n\\) is odd and \\(n\\ge5\\), the possible values are again \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\);  \n* for the special case \\(n=3\\) the values are \\(0,1,2,3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound for a sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on it, then \\(b=ma+b\\) gives \\(b-ma=b\\).  \n   Because \\(a,b\\) are integers, \\(m\\) must be rational; write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms (\\(q>0\\)).  \n   The condition \\(b=pa/q+b\\) forces \\(q\\mid a\\).  \n   Hence the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on a fixed sunny line are spaced at least \\(q\\) units apart.  \n   Since \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such points can appear.  \n   The smallest possible denominator is \\(q=2\\) (the slope \\(\\pm\\frac12\\) or any other non‑integer rational with denominator \\(2\\)).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\bigl(L\\cap T_n\\bigr)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is attained by the line of slope \\(1\\) (e.g. \\(y=x\\)), which contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of points they could cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is even*, write \\(n=2m\\). Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and (2) equals \\(2m^{2}=n^{2}/2\\).  \n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2=m(2m+1)=2m^{2}+m\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n^{2}}2\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   so covering all points with only sunny lines is impossible.\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is odd and \\(n\\ge5\\)*, write \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).  \n   Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and (2) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   (2m+1)(m+1)=2m^{2}+3m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2=(2m+1)(m+1)=2m^{2}+3m+1\\), equality in (2) can hold only if **every** sunny line contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The line \\(y=x\\) indeed contains \\(m+1\\) points, but any sunny line with slope different from \\(1\\) contains **strictly fewer** than \\(m+1\\) points.  \n   To see this, let \\(L\\) have slope \\(p/q\\) with \\(|p|\\neq|q|\\) and \\(q\\ge2\\).  \n   From the spacing argument above, the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) are at distance at least \\(q\\ge2\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#(L\\cap T_n)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=m< m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence at most one line can achieve the maximal size \\(m+1\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would together cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)m = (2m)(m)=2m^{2}<|T_n|- (m+1),\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting the requirement to cover all points.  \n   Consequently **\\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\).**\n\n4. **Existence for all \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n\n   *Sunny lines.* For each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) take  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x+i .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny and are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.* For each \\(j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) take  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   These are vertical, hence non‑sunny, and are distinct.\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *Case 1.* If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_{\\,b-a}\\) from (3).\n\n   *Case 2.* If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-(b-a)}2\\le\\frac{n+1-k}2\\le n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-k\\); consequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) from (4) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   In both cases the point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, so the covering condition holds.\n\n   The families (3) and (4) are disjoint, therefore the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\) and the total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n5. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the construction above gives admissible values \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \n   In addition, the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, none is parallel to the forbidden directions, and each contains exactly two points of \\(T_3\\); together they cover all six points of \\(T_3\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=3\\) is also possible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   * For every even \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values are \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n   * For every odd \\(n\\ge5\\) the admissible values are again \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(0,1,2,3\\).\n\n   This can be expressed compactly as  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\text{ for all }n\\ge3,\\ \\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A bound for a single sunny line  \n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on it then  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+b\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b-ma=b .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are integers, \\(m\\) must be rational; write \\(m=p/q\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(q>0\\).  The equality becomes  \n\n\\[\nqb=pa+qb\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q\\mid a .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(T_n\\) that belong to this line are\ncongruent to \\(0\\pmod q\\); they are spaced at least \\(q\\) units apart.\nBecause \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/q\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such\n\\(a\\)’s exist.  The smallest possible denominator for a non‑horizontal,\nnon‑\\(-1\\) slope is \\(q=2\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap T_n\\bigr)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n      =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) attains the bound, because its points in\n\\(T_n\\) are \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\n\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of using only sunny lines when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) required lines are sunny.  \nFrom (6) each line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of points that can be covered\nis at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Even \\(n\\).*  Write \\(n=2m\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\)\nand (7) equals \\(2m^{2}=n^{2}/2\\).  By (5),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=m(2m+1)=2m^{2}+m>2m^{2},\n\\]\n\nso (7) is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|\\).  Hence covering all points with\nonly sunny lines is impossible for every even \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n*Odd \\(n\\ge5\\).*  Write \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).  Then\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and (7) equals  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(m+1)=2m^{2}+3m+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (5) we have \\(|T_n|=2m^{2}+3m+1\\); thus equality in (7) can hold\nonly if **every** sunny line contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) indeed contains \\(m+1\\) points.  \nConsider any other sunny line \\(L\\) with slope \\(p/q\\) where \\(|p|\\neq|q|\\) and\n\\(q\\ge2\\).  By the spacing argument above the \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of\n\\(L\\cap T_n\\) differ by at least \\(q\\ge2\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\#(L\\cap T_n)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=m<m+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus at most one line can achieve the maximal size \\(m+1\\); the remaining\n\\(n-1\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-1)m=2m^{2}\\) points, which is\nstrictly less than \\(|T_n|-(m+1)\\).  Hence it is impossible to cover all\npoints with \\(n\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge4\\text{ the value }k=n\\text{ is impossible}.}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.* For each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=x+i .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(S_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny and are pairwise\ndifferent.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.* For each \\(j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical, hence non‑sunny, and are distinct.\n\n*Coverage proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of\n\\(S_{\\,b-a}\\) from (10).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\ge k\\)*, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-(b-a)}2\\le\\frac{n+1-k}2\\le n-k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-k\\); consequently the vertical\nline \\(V_a\\) from (11) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) lines.\nThe families (10) and (11) are disjoint, so exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are\nsunny.\n\nTherefore for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the construction above yields admissible values \\(k=0,1,2\\).\nIn addition, the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none is parallel to the forbidden directions, and each contains\nexactly two points of  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nExplicitly,\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{line} & \\text{points of }T_3\\text{ on it}\\\\ \\hline\ny=x & (1,1),\\;(2,2)\\\\\ny=-\\frac12x+\\frac52 & (1,2),\\;(3,1)\\\\\ny=-2x+5 & (1,3),\\;(2,1)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nso all six points are covered.  Hence \\(k=3\\) is also possible when\n\\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final classification  \n\nCombining the impossibility result (9) with the construction of §3 and the\nspecial example for \\(n=3\\), we obtain the complete description:\n\n* **If \\(n\\) is even** (\\(n\\ge4\\)): the possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n  \\[\n  k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n* **If \\(n\\) is odd and \\(n\\ge5\\)**: the same set \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) is\n  attainable, while \\(k=n\\) is impossible by the argument of §2.\n\n* **If \\(n=3\\)**: the attainable values are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\nIn compact form,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\text{ for all }n\\ge3,\\quad\\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3\\; } .\n\\]"
