e5\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Verification Log**\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f1 \u2013 Definition of the required point set  \n\n> \u201c\\[\n> T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n> \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nThe conditions exactly match the problem statement. Counting the integer solutions of \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]  \nso (1) is correct. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2 \u2013 Maximum number of points on a sunny line  \n\n#### 2.1  Representation of a sunny line  \n\n> \u201cWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nA sunny line is defined as not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(m\\neq0\\)), not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (non\u2011vertical, so a slope exists), and not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). Hence the representation is valid.\n\n#### 2.2  Rational slope case  \n\n> \u201cIf \\(m\\) is rational, write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms (\\(q>0\\)).  \n> The condition that \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line forces  \n> \\[\n> b=\\frac{p}{q}a+\\frac{r}{q}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q\\mid(p a+r).\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:*  \nThe step tacitly assumes that the intercept \\(c\\) can be written as \\(r/q\\) with integer \\(r\\). A rigorous argument is needed: for integer \\(a,b\\) satisfying \\(b= (p/q)a + c\\), we have \\(c = b - (p/q)a = (qb - pa)/q\\), so indeed \\(c\\) is rational with denominator dividing \\(q\\). The solution omits this justification.\n\n*Correct continuation (assuming the gap is filled):*  \nFrom \\(qb = pa + r\\) we obtain the congruence \\(pa \\equiv -r \\pmod q\\). Since \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), this is equivalent to a single congruence class \\(a\\equiv a_0\\pmod q\\). Hence among the integers \\(1,\\dots,n\\) there are at most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\) admissible abscissas.\n\n#### 2.3  Bounding the number of points for \\(q\\ge2\\)  \n\n> \u201cHence the admissible abscissas satisfy a single congruence \\(a\\equiv a_0\\pmod q\\); among the integers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) there are at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/q\\bigr\\rceil\\) such values.  \n> For \\(q\\ge2\\) this gives  \n> \\[\n> \\#\\bigl(L\\cap T_n\\bigr)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nFor \\(q\\ge2\\) the worst case is \\(q=2\\), giving \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil = \\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\). The bound is correct.\n\n#### 2.4  Integer\u2011slope case  \n\n> \u201cIf \\(m\\) is an integer (\\(q=1\\)), the only integer slopes that are not forbidden are \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\).\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nCorrect; the forbidden integer slopes are \\(0\\) (horizontal) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n##### 2.4.1  Slope \\(m=1\\)  \n\n> \u201cFor \\(m=1\\) the line is \\(y=x+c\\); it contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\), and the maximum (over all integer \\(c\\)) equals \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nFrom \\(b=a+c\\) and the constraints \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(2a\\le n+1-c\\). Hence the number of admissible \\(a\\) is \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (after checking the lower bound \\(a\\ge\\max\\{1,1-c\\}\\)). The maximum occurs at \\(c=0\\), giving \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). This is correct.\n\n##### 2.4.2  Slopes \\(|m|\\ge2\\)  \n\n> \u201cFor any other integer slope \\(|m|\\ge2\\) the inequality \\((m+1)a\\le n+1-c\\) (or its analogue for \\(m\\le-2\\)) shows that at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|m|+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points can lie on the line, which is strictly smaller than the bound in (3) for \\(n\\ge3\\).\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nFrom \\(b=ma+c\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get \\((m+1)a\\le n+1-c\\). Since \\(|m|+1\\ge3\\), the bound \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/(|m|+1)\\rfloor\\) is at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/3\\rfloor\\), which is indeed smaller than \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\). The argument is sound.\n\n#### 2.5  Summary of the bound  \n\n> \u201cConsequently **every sunny line contains at most**  \n> \\[\n> M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n> \\]  \n> points of \\(T_n\\), and the bound is attained only by lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\).\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:*  \nThe bound \\(M\\) is correct, but the claim \u201cattained only by lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\)\u201d is too broad. In fact, only the line \\(y=x\\) (slope\u202f\\(1\\) with intercept\u202f\\(0\\)) attains \\(M\\); any other slope\u2011\\(1\\) line \\(y=x+i\\) with \\(i\\neq0\\) contains fewer points. The statement should be refined. The gap does not affect later arguments (which later correctly identify \\(y=x\\) as the unique maximal line), but the wording is imprecise.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f3 \u2013 Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n#### 3.1  General counting argument  \n\n> \u201cIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the number of *distinct* points they could cover is at most \\(nM\\).\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nSince each sunny line contains at most \\(M\\) points, the union of \\(n\\) such lines contains at most \\(nM\\) points. Correct.\n\n#### 3.2  Even \\(n\\)  \n\n> \u201cEven \\(n\\). Write \\(n=2m\\). Then \\(M=m\\) and \\(nM=2m^{2}\\).  \n> From (1) \\(|T_n|=2m^{2}+m>2m^{2}\\); therefore the covering is impossible.\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nFor even \\(n=2m\\), \\(M=\\lfloor (2m+1)/2\\rfloor=m\\). Hence \\(nM=2m^{2}\\). Since \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2=m(2m+1)=2m^{2}+m\\), we have \\(|T_n|>nM\\). Thus covering all points with only sunny lines is impossible. The argument is correct.\n\n#### 3.3  Odd \\(n\\ge5\\) \u2013 initial observations  \n\n> \u201cOdd \\(n\\ge5\\). Write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)). Then \\(M=m+1\\) and \\(nM=(2m+1)(m+1)=|T_n|\\).  \n> Equality can hold only if **every** sunny line contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points and the sets of points are pairwise disjoint.\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nFor odd \\(n=2m+1\\), \\(M=\\lfloor (2m+2)/2\\rfloor=m+1\\). Hence \\(nM=(2m+1)(m+1)=|T_n|\\). If the union of the \\(n\\) lines is to cover all \\(|T_n|\\) points, each line must achieve the maximal size \\(M\\) and the point sets must be disjoint; otherwise the total would be strictly less than \\(|T_n|\\). This reasoning is sound.\n\n#### 3.4  Which sunny lines can achieve the maximal size?  \n\n> \u201cBy the discussion in step\u202f2 the only sunny lines that can contain \\(m+1\\) points are the lines \\(y=x+c\\) with \\(c=0\\); all other sunny lines contain at most \\(m\\) points.\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nFrom step\u202f2 we know that a slope\u2011\\(1\\) line attains \\(M\\) only when \\(c=0\\); any other slope\u2011\\(1\\) line or any line of different slope contains at most \\(m\\) points. Hence the claim is correct.\n\n#### 3.5  Counting the points when at most one line is maximal  \n\n> \u201cHence at most one line can achieve the maximal size, so the total number of points covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n> \\[\n> (m+1)+(n-1)m = (2m+1)m + (m+1)=|T_n|-(m+1) < |T_n|.\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Issue \u2013 Critical Error:*  \nThe algebraic manipulation is incorrect.\n\n- Correct computation:  \n  \\[\n  (m+1)+(n-1)m = (m+1)+(2m+1-1)m = (m+1)+2m^{2}=2m^{2}+m+1.\n  \\]\n\n- The expression written in the solution, \\((2m+1)m+(m+1)\\), equals \\(2m^{2}+2m+1\\), which is **not** equal to the previous line and is **not** equal to \\(|T_n|-(m+1)\\).  \n\n- Since \\(|T_n| = (2m+1)(m+1)=2m^{2}+3m+1\\), the correct difference is \\(|T_n|-(2m^{2}+m+1)=2m\\), not \\((m+1)\\).\n\nThus the statement \u201c\\(=|T_n|-(m+1)\\)\u201d is false. This miscalculation breaks the logical chain that concludes the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\). Consequently the impossibility proof for odd \\(n\\) is not established.\n\n*Impact:* Because the impossibility claim for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) rests on this faulty inequality, the proof of \u201c\\(k=n\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge4\\)\u201d is incomplete. This constitutes a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f4 \u2013 Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\n> \u201cLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n> *Sunny lines.* For each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define \\(S_i:\\;y=x+i\\).  \n> *Non\u2011sunny lines.* For each \\(j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) define \\(V_j:\\;x=j\\).\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \n- Each \\(S_i\\) has slope\u202f\\(1\\), hence is sunny (not parallel to the forbidden directions).  \n- Each \\(V_j\\) is vertical, thus non\u2011sunny.  \n- All lines are distinct because the \\(S_i\\) have distinct intercepts and the \\(V_j\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates.\n\n> \u201cTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n> - **Case\u202f1.** If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{b-a}\\).  \n> - **Case\u202f2.** If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we obtain  \n>   \\[\n>   a\\le\\frac{n+1-(b-a)}2\\le\\frac{n+1-k}2\\le n-k .\n>   \\]  \n>   Hence \\(a\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\); consequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \n- **Case\u202f1** is immediate from the definition of \\(S_i\\).  \n- In **Case\u202f2**, the chain of inequalities is valid: from \\(b-a\\ge k\\) we have \\(b\\ge a+k\\); together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) this yields \\(2a\\le n+1-k\\), i.e. \\(a\\le (n+1-k)/2\\). Since \\(k\\le n-1\\), we have \\(n+1-k\\ge2\\) and thus \\((n+1-k)/2\\le n-k\\) (equivalent to \\(1\\le n-k\\)). Hence \\(a\\) indeed lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots,n-k\\}\\), so the vertical line \\(x=a\\) covers the point.  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines. The families are disjoint, giving exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. The construction is correct.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f5 \u2013 Exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\n> \u201cThe construction above yields admissible values \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \n> In addition, the three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n> L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n> L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n> \\]  \n> are distinct, none is parallel to the forbidden directions, and they cover all six points of  \n> \\[\n> T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \n- Slopes \\(1\\), \\(-\\tfrac12\\), and \\(-2\\) are all different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\), and undefined, so all three lines are sunny.  \n- Substituting the six points confirms the table given in the solution:\n  - \\(L_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\);\n  - \\(L_2\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\);\n  - \\(L_3\\) contains \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).\n- Hence every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on a sunny line, giving a configuration with \\(k=3\\).\n\nThe special construction is correct.\n\n---\n\n### Overall assessment\n\nThe construction part (Steps\u202f4 and\u202f5) is rigorous and correct.  \nThe counting argument for even \\(n\\) (Step\u202f3, even case) is also correct.  \n\nHowever, the impossibility proof for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) contains a **Critical Error** (incorrect algebraic manipulation) that invalidates the conclusion that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for odd \\(n\\ge5\\). Because the final answer relies on this impossibility claim, the solution as a whole is not a valid proof.  \n\nThe two minor **Justification Gaps** identified in Step\u202f2 do not affect the final result but should be noted for completeness.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The solution is not correct due to the critical error in the impossibility argument for odd \\(n\\ge5\\)."

