 \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n     The maximum is attained **only** when \\(c=0\\), i.e. for the line\n     \\(y=x\\).\n\n     *For \\(|m|\\ge2\\)* the inequality\n     \\((|m|+1)a\\le n+1-|c|\\) shows that at most\n     \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|m|+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n     points can lie on the line, which is again bounded by (3).\n\n   Consequently every sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\), and the bound \\(M\\) is achieved **only** by the single\n   line \\(y=x\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny when \\(n\\ge4\\) (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n\n   Suppose all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   The total number of *distinct* points they can cover is at most \\(nM\\).\n\n   *Even \\(n=2m\\).*  \n   Then \\(M=m\\) and \\(nM=2m^{2}\\).  \n   From (1) we have \\(|T_n|=2m^{2}+m>2m^{2}=nM\\); thus covering all points is\n   impossible.\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge5\\)).*  \n   Here \\(M=m+1\\) and \\(nM=(2m+1)(m+1)=|T_n|\\).  \n   Equality could hold only if **every** sunny line contains exactly \\(M\\)\n   points and the point\u2011sets of the lines are pairwise disjoint.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 the only sunny line that can contain \\(M\\) points is \\(y=x\\); all\n   other sunny lines contain at most \\(m\\) points.  \n   Hence the total number of points covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (m+1)+(n-1)m=(m+1)+(2m)(m)=2m^{2}+m+1\n   =|T_n|-2m<|T_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Therefore for every \\(n\\ge5\\) it is impossible to have\n   \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n   For \\(n=3\\) (the case \\(m=1\\)) the above counting gives equality, and a\n   concrete example with three sunny lines will be exhibited later; thus\n   \\(k=3\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\) (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n\n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Define the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n   *Sunny lines* (slope \\(1\\)):\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines* (vertical):\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The families (5) and (6) are disjoint, so exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are\n   sunny.\n\n   **Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n   constructed lines.**  \n   For a given point set \\(d:=b-a\\).\n\n   - **Case\u202f1.** \\(d\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \n     Then \\(b-a=i\\) for some \\(i\\) in that set, and the point satisfies the\n     equation of the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\n   - **Case\u202f2.** \\(d\\ge k\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     a\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k .\n     \\]\n\n     The first inequality uses \\(d\\ge k\\); the second uses the elementary\n     bound \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\) which holds for all \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n     Since also \\(a\\ge1\\), we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), and therefore the\n     vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   In both cases the point belongs to at least one of the lines, completing\n   the covering requirement.\n\n5. **The exceptional case \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).**  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the\n   \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Direct substitution shows the incidence table\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{line} & \\text{points of }T_3\\text{ on it}\\\\ \\hline\n   y=x & (1,1),\\;(2,2)\\\\\n   y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 & (1,2),\\;(3,1)\\\\\n   y=-2x+5 & (1,3),\\;(2,1)\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence all six points are covered and exactly three sunny lines are used.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\); Lemma\u202f3\n   provides a construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\); the explicit\n   example above handles the remaining possibility \\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\).  \n   Therefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\text{ for all }n\\ge3,\\quad\\text{and also }k=n\\text{ when }n=3\\; } .\n   \\]"
