) and \\(nM=|T_n|\\).  Consequently every point of\n   \\(T_n\\) must be covered **exactly once**; otherwise the total number of\n   incidences would be \\(<|T_n|\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines must be pairwise\n   disjoint on \\(T_n\\).\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can contain \\(M\\) points only if it is of one of the\n   two types listed in (3).  The lines of type \\(y=x+c\\) are parallel, so they\n   are automatically disjoint on \\(T_n\\).  The line \\(x+y=M+1\\) meets each of\n   the parallel lines in exactly one point of \\(T_n\\); therefore it cannot be\n   used together with any of them if the families are to be disjoint.\n\n   Hence at most \\(M\\) parallel lines of type \\(y=x+c\\) can be taken, and the\n   line \\(x+y=M+1\\) can be taken at most **once**.  Consequently the total number\n   of sunny lines that can be pairwise disjoint on \\(T_n\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M+1 = (m+1)+1 = m+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n=2m+1>m+2\\) for every \\(m\\ge2\\), we cannot obtain \\(n\\) pairwise\n   disjoint sunny lines, and the covering is impossible.\n\n   For the remaining odd case \\(n=3\\) (\\(m=1\\)) we have \\(M=2\\) and\n   \\(nM=|T_3|=6\\).  In this special size the three sunny lines displayed in\n   Lemma\u202f3 below do cover all points, so \\(k=n\\) is possible only when\n   \\(n=3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\) (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Define  \n\n   * sunny lines (slope\u202f1)  \n     \\[\n     S_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1);\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]  \n   * non\u2011sunny vertical lines  \n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   The families (4) and (5) are pairwise distinct, so exactly \\(k\\) of the\n   \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   **Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n   constructed lines.**  \n   Put \\(d:=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f1.* \\(d\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \n   Then \\(b-a=i\\) for some 