t most \\(\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\) and whose\n   projection on the \\(y\\)-axis has length at most \\(\\frac{n-1}{|1/p|}=|p|(n-1)\\).  \n   Hence the number of integer points on the segment is bounded by the\n   length of the projection onto the direction \\((1,1)\\), i.e. by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n-1}{|p+1|}+1\\le\\frac{n-1}{1}+1=n\\le 2M .\n   \\]\n\n   A sharper bound is obtained by observing that any integer point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)\n   satisfies \\(0\\le b-a\\le n-1\\).  Along a sunny line the quantity \\(b-a\\) varies\n   strictly monotonically because the slope is not \\(1\\).  Consequently the set\n   \\(\\{b-a\\mid (a,b)\\in T_n\\cap\\ell\\}\\) is an interval of consecutive integers of\n   length at most \\(M\\).  Hence \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le M\\).  \n   Equality forces the slope to be \\(+1\\); otherwise the monotonicity would give a\n   strictly smaller interval.  For slope \\(+1\\) the line is \\(y=x+c\\) and the\n   condition \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) becomes \\(c=b-a\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le n-1\\) and\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  The latter inequality is equivalent to \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\),\n   which yields exactly \\(M\\) admissible values of \\(a\\) when \\(0\\le c\\le M-1\\).\n   ∎\n\n3. **Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny when \\(n\\ge4\\) (Lemma 2).**  \n\n   *Even \\(n=2m\\).*  \n   Here \\(M=m\\).  Each sunny line contributes at most \\(m\\) incidences with points of\n   \\(T_n\\); with \\(n\\) sunny lines the total number of incidences is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   nM = 2m\\cdot m = 2m^{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = m(2m+1)=|T_n|.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence covering all points of \\(T_n\\) is impossible.\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge5\\)).*  \n   Now \\(M=m+1\\) and \\(nM=|T_n|\\).  Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) must be\n   incident to **exactly one** of the sunny lines; otherwise the total number of\n   incidences would be \\(<|T_n|\\).  Consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines are pairwise\n   disjoint on \\(T_n\\).\n\n   By Lemma 1 a sunny line can contain \\(M\\) points only if it is of the form\n   \\(y=x+c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le M-1\\).  All such lines are parallel, so they are\n   automatically disjoint on \\(T_n\\).  Any other sunny line (with slope \\(\\neq1\\))\n   contains at most \\(M-1\\) points, and therefore would intersect one of the\n   parallel lines in at least one point of \\(T_n\\), contradicting the required\n   disjointness.  Hence **all** \\(n\\) sunny lines would have to be among the\n   parallel family \\(\\{y=x+c\\mid 0\\le c\\le M-1\\}\\).  This family contains only\n   \\(M\\) distinct lines, but \\(n=2m+1>m+1=M\\) for every \\(m\\ge2\\).  Thus a covering\n   with \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist for any odd \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n   *The remaining odd case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   Here \\(M=2\\) and \\(nM=|T_3|=6\\).  Equality again forces the three sunny lines to\n   be pairwise disjoint on \\(T_3\\).  The three lines displayed in Lemma 4 below\n   satisfy this requirement, so \\(k=n=3\\) is possible only for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   Consequently **\\(k=n\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\); for every \\(n\\ge4\\) it is\n   impossible.**\n\n4. **Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\) (Lemma 3).**  \n\n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Define  \n\n   * sunny lines (slope 1)  \n     \\[\n     S_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1);\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]  \n   * non‑sunny vertical lines  \n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n   The families (4) and (5) consist of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\)\n   of which are sunny.\n\n   **Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n   constructed lines.**  \n   Set \\(d:=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1.* \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).  \n   Then \\(b-a=d\\) for some \\(d\\) in the index set of (4), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies\n   the equation of the sunny line \\(S_d\\).\n\n   *Case 2.* \\(d\\ge k\\).  \n   From the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The first inequality uses \\(d\\ge k\\); the second uses the elementary fact that\n   for any integers \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\n   \\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\n   n-1\\ge0 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Since also \\(a\\ge1\\), we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), and therefore the\n   vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   In both cases the point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines,\n   so the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n5. **The exceptional case \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\) (Lemma 4).**  \n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the\n   \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Direct substitution gives\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{line} & \\text{points of }T_3\\text{ on it}\\\\ \\hline\n   y=x & (1,1),\\;(2,2)\\\\\n   y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 & (1,2),\\;(3,1)\\\\\n   y=-2x+5 & (1,3),\\;(2,1)\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of the three sunny lines,\n   and exactly three sunny lines are used.\n\n6. **The case \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\) is impossible (Lemma 5).**  \n\n   Suppose three distinct lines cover \\(T_3\\) with exactly two sunny lines.\n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line can contain at most \\(M=2\\) points of \\(T_3\\); the\n   non‑sunny line, being vertical or horizontal, can contain at most three\n   points (it would have to be \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) to meet the condition\n   \\(a+b\\le4\\)).  Counting incidences we obtain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2+3=7\n   \\]\n\n   possible point‑line incidences, but the six points of \\(T_3\\) must each be\n   incident to **at least one** line.  If any point were incident to two lines,\n   the total number of incidences would be at most \\(6\\), contradicting the\n   necessity of covering all six points.  Hence each point must be incident to\n   exactly one of the three lines.  Consequently the two sunny lines must be\n   disjoint on \\(T_3\\).  By Lemma 1 the only sunny lines attaining two points are\n   \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=x+1\\); however these two lines intersect at \\((1,1)\\), so they\n   are not disjoint.  Any other sunny line contains at most one point of\n   \\(T_3\\), making it impossible to achieve the required six distinct incidences.\n   Therefore no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\); Lemma 5 shows that\n   \\(k=n-1\\) is also impossible when \\(n=3\\).  Lemma 3 supplies a construction\n   for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (including the case \\(k=0\\)).  The\n   explicit example (8) handles the remaining feasible value \\(k=n\\) when\n   \\(n=3\\).  Hence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
