ical line meets a column in **at most one** point.\n   Hence the points of the columns that are **not** equipped with a vertical\n   line must be supplied one‑by‑one by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   If the set of such columns has size \\(m=n-v\\) then, even in the most\n   favourable situation (the columns are the right‑most ones, i.e. the\n   smallest columns), we need at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m(m+1)}2\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct non‑vertical lines to cover them.\n   Because we have exactly \\(k=m\\) non‑vertical lines, (3) forces\n   \\(m\\le1\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le1\\qquad\\text{whenever }n\\ge4 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lemma 4 (existence for the admissible values).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, none of them is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on\n   the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\)).*  \n   Take the sunny line through the two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its\n   equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y-1=-\\frac1{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac1{n-1}\\bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence it is\n   on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\) (hence \\((a,b)=(n,1)\\) and lies\n   on \\(L\\)).  The point \\((1,2)\\) belongs to both \\(L\\) and the line\n   \\(x=1\\); all other points are covered exactly once.  Thus a covering with\n   exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n   *\\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the\n   \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  The six points of \\(T_3\\) are\n\n   \\[\n   (1,1