ight\u2011most \\(k\\) columns\n   \\(\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); this choice minimises the number of points that must be supplied by sunny lines.\n   The total number of points lying in those columns equals  \n\n   \\[\n   P_k=\\sum_{t=1}^{k} t=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contains at most \\(S=\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\); in particular it can meet at most \\(S\\) of the uncovered columns.  \n   Since \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\) we have \\(S\\le n-2\\), so a single sunny line can meet at most \\(k-1\\) of the uncovered columns when \\(k\\ge2\\).  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(k(k-1)\\) points of the set of uncovered columns.  \n   For \\(k\\ge2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   k(k-1)<\\frac{k(k+1)}2 ,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the sunny lines cannot supply enough points to cover all points of the uncovered columns.  \n   This contradiction shows that no covering with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists when \\(n\\ge4\\). \u220e\n\n6. **Lemma\u202f5 (existence for the admissible values).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\)).*  \n   The vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, none is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\)).*  \n   Take the sunny line through the two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y-1=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n) .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\) (the only such point is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(L\\)).  Thus all points are covered and 