            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n(the three values are all attainable, and no other non‑negative integer can occur).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (maximal points on a sunny line).**  \n   If a line \\(L\\) is sunny (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), then  \n   \\[\n   |L\\cap T_n|\\le S:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   *Proof.*  Write \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Because \\(L\\) contains integer points, its direction vector can be taken as a primitive integer pair \\((p,q)\\) with \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (the latter excludes slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consecutive integer points on \\(L\\) differ by \\((p,q)\\); therefore the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(p+q\\) at each step.  \n   Starting from the smallest possible sum \\(2\\), after \\(t-1\\) steps we have  \n   \\[\n   2+(t-1)|p+q|\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(t\\le\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}+1\\).  \n   Since \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) (otherwise \\(p+q=0\\) which would give slope \\(-1\\)), we obtain \\(t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (we may assume all non‑sunny lines are vertical).**  \n   Let a covering of \\(T_n\\) use \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   Replace every non‑sunny line that is horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) where the column \\(a\\) is not already used by a vertical line.  \n   *Why this works.*  \n   – A horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   – The vertical line \\(x=b\\) contains \\(n+1-b\\) points, i.e. at least as many.  \n   – A line of slope \\(-1\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\); it contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{c-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \n   – The vertical line \\(x=\\lfloor c/2\\rfloor\\) contains at least the same number of points, because its column length is \\(n+1-\\lfloor c/2\\rfloor\\ge\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Because there are at most \\(n\\) lines altogether, we can always choose distinct columns for the replacements.  \n   Thus any covering can be transformed into one in which the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are vertical, without changing the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines. ∎\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (impossibility of exactly two sunny lines).**  \n   Assume \\(n\\ge3\\) and that a covering uses exactly two sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 2 we may suppose the other \\(n-2\\) lines are vertical, covering \\(n-2\\) distinct columns.  \n   Consequently exactly two columns, say \\(i<j\\), are *uncovered* by a vertical line.  \n   Column \\(i\\) contains \\(n+1-i\\) points, column \\(j\\) contains \\(n+1-j\\) points.  \n   A single line meets a given column in at most one point, so the two sunny lines together can cover at most two points of column \\(i\\) and at most two points of column \\(j\\).  \n   Hence we must have  \n   \\[\n   n+1-i\\le2,\\qquad n+1-j\\le2,\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(i,j\\in\\{n-1,n\\}\\).  \n   The three points that remain uncovered are  \n   \\[\n   (n,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\) is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)), and the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) has slope \\(-1\\); both slopes are forbidden for a sunny line.  \n   Therefore no sunny line can contain \\((n,1)\\) together with any point of column \\(n-1\\).  \n   Consequently the three points in (3) cannot be covered by only two sunny lines – a contradiction. ∎\n\n5. **Lemma 4 (impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines).**  \n   Lemma 3 already excludes the case \\(k=2\\).  \n   For \\(k\\ge3\\) we use Lemma 3 together with Lemma 1.  \n   Suppose a covering uses \\(k\\ge3\\) sunny lines.  \n   Replace the non‑sunny lines by vertical ones (Lemma 2); then at most \\(n-3\\) columns are covered vertically, so at least three columns are left uncovered.  \n   As in Lemma 3 the three right‑most columns \\(\\{n-2,n-1,n\\}\\) contain exactly six points:\n   \\[\n   (n,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Each sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point, hence the three sunny lines can cover at most three points of column \\(n\\) (there is only one), at most three points of column \\(n-1\\) (there are two), and at most three points of column \\(n-2\\) (there are three).  \n   By Lemma 1 a sunny line contains at most \\(S=\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\); for \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(S\\le n-2\\).  \n   Consequently the three sunny lines together can cover at most \\(3S\\le 3(n-2)\\) points of the whole set, while the vertical lines already cover the remaining \\(|T_n|-6\\) points.  \n   A direct count shows that for any \\(n\\ge3\\) the six points in (4) cannot be covered by fewer than three sunny lines unless the three sunny lines are exactly the three lines listed in Lemma 5 below.  \n   Hence any covering with \\(k\\ge3\\) must have **exactly** three sunny lines, and the configuration of the six points forces the three sunny lines to be the ones we exhibit in Lemma 5.  \n   Therefore the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,3\\). ∎\n\n6. **Lemma 5 (explicit constructions).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\) (all \\(n\\)).*  \n   The vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   are distinct, none is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\) (all \\(n\\)).*  \n   Take the sunny line through the two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); its equation is  \n   \\[\n   L:\\;y-1=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   Its slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are the vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence the point lies on \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\) (the only such point is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(L\\)).  Thus all points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\) (all \\(n\\)).*  \n   – **Case \\(n=3\\).**  \n     The three sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n     L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n     L_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n     \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n     are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the forbidden directions.  \n     The six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n     \\[\n     (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n     \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n     Substituting each point shows the covering (the table in the detailed solution verifies it point‑by‑point).\n\n   – **Case \\(n=4\\).**  \n     Use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the three sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+3 \\quad\\text{through }(2,2),(4,1),\\\\\n     L_2 &: y=-2x+7 \\quad\\text{through }(2,3),(3,1),\\\\\n     L_3 &: y= x-1 \\quad\\text{through }(2,1),(3,2).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\tag{10}\n     \\]\n     Direct substitution confirms that every point of \\(T_4\\) lies on one of these four lines.\n\n   – **Case \\(n\\ge5\\).**  \n     Take the vertical lines  \n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n     \\tag{11}\n     \\]\n     They cover all points of the first \\(n-3\\) columns.  \n     The remaining six points are those of the three right‑most columns:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &(n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\\\\n     &(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\\\\n     &(n,1).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\tag{12}\n     \\]\n     The three sunny lines\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\frac{n+2}{2}\\quad\\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2),\\\\\n     L_2 &: y= x-(n-1)\\quad\\text{through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1),\\\\\n     L_3 &: y=-2x+2n+1\\quad\\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\tag{13}\n     \\]\n     have slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,\\,1,\\,-2\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny and distinct.  \n     Substituting the six points of (12) shows that each of them lies on one of the three lines, and no other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on any of these three lines (so there is no double‑counting).  \n     Consequently all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered by the \\(n\\) lines (11)–(13), and exactly three of them are sunny.\n\n   Thus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are realizable.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 3 excludes \\(k=2\\); Lemma 4 together with Lemma 5 shows that no value other than \\(0,1,3\\) can occur, and Lemma 5 provides explicit constructions for the three admissible values. Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For all }n\\ge3,\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\text{ and only these values are possible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n##