a+b=c\\)). The bound \\(\\lfloor\\frac{c-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) is **incorrect**.  \n     - Consequently the claim that a vertical line \\(x=\\lfloor c/2\\rfloor\\) always covers at least as many points is false; for example with \\(n=5,\\;c=6\\) the slope‑\\(-1\\) line has 5 points while the vertical line \\(x=3\\) has only 3.  \n   - *Issue:* The entire replacement argument collapses. → **Critical Error**.\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (impossibility of exactly two sunny lines).**  \n   - *Quote:* “By Lemma 2 we may suppose the other \\(n-2\\) lines are vertical… Consequently exactly two columns, say \\(i<j\\), are uncovered… the three points that remain uncovered are \\((n,1),(n-1,1),(n-1,2)\\).”  \n   - *Verification:* This lemma **depends** on Lemma 2. Since Lemma 2 is false, the reduction to “all non‑sunny lines are vertical” is unjustified, and the subsequent counting argument is unsupported. → **Critical Error** (propagated from Lemma 2).\n\n5. **Lemma 4 (impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines).**  \n   - *Quote:* “Lemma 3 already excludes the case \\(k=2\\). For \\(k\\ge3\\) we use Lemma 3 together with Lemma 1… we may assume the non‑sunny lines are vertical (Lemma 2).”  \n   - *Verification:* Again relies on Lemma 2 (critical error) and on Lemma 1 (which has a justification gap). Hence the conclusion that no \\(k\\ge2\\) (except possibly \\(k=3\\)) is possible is **not proved**. → **Critical Error**.\n\n6. **Lemma 5 (explicit constructions).**  \n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  \n     - *Quote:* “The vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots,x=n\\) … cover all points.”  \n     - *Verification:* Correct. Each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and lies on \\(x=a\\). No issue.  \n\n   - **\\(k=1\\).**  \n     - *Quote:* “Take the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are \\(x=1,\\dots,x=n-1\\).”  \n     - *Verification:* The line has slope \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny. The vertical lines cover all points with \\(a\\le